Janis Papanagnou <Janis_Papanagnou@hotmail.com> wrote:

>The 1000+ experience points when killing
>sea creatures that can drown you is for the added risk you take in this
>case. A sea creature on dry land is no risk at all and, furthermore, if
>you just wait their HP's will even drop to 1 HP. You get 1000+ Xp for
>just 1 HP effort!

Sea creatures are not generated on land. If one uses up a charge of a
wand of teleportation to teleport a kraken onto dry land and kill it
there, it is as good a way of killing it as zapping it with magic
missiles from afar, which also protects against drowning. Why should the
hero get less experience for any such tactic?

In a similar way, cockatrices are not deadly for characters with a lizard
corpse, orcs with poisoned arrows pose less threat to poison resistant
characters; experience points should not lessen for good preparation.

(If one wants to lessen the benefits of reverse genociding krakens on
land, a better solution would be to decrease the value of sent-in
creatures in general than just to limit experience points in this special
case. I do not consider this a worthwile addition.)
